I doubt it. Suppose the published prospectus to the university had
some pages containing comments from students, e.g. on why they had
chosen the university etc. And suppose the one of the students
wrote something libellous about a previous employer.

I doubt that the university would be covered against being sued
simply because

(a) it had a statement disclaiming responsibility for statements in
the student section

(b) students had signed a document, etc....

Of course, if a student had violated some contract then he/or she
could be sued by the university. But how many students would be able
to pay damages on a scale to match the damages the University might
have to pay to another organisation? I doubt that many of them have
insurance cover for this kind of thing.

I don't know if the two situations (prospectus and web pages) are
exactly analogous because of course the university would actually
print and circulate copies of the prospectus, whereas a web page is
more like a notice put up in the window for others to view from
outside with their telescopes. But we are all legal amateurs here
and we'll have to take advice from the professionals.

My suspicion is that we probably cannot cover the university fully
legally, so we have to take steps to ensure that before students are
given the right to make their web pages externally visible they must
also sign a document saying that they understand the potential
seriousness of .... etc. etc.

This way we shall not cover ourselves legally, but we may reduce the
risk of students being irresponsible, either maliciously or through
thoughtlessnes.

I have seen enough examples of both in my time to know that we have
to do something to reduce the risks. It's a delicate balancing act.

Aaron
