The amended Telecommunications Competition and Deregulation Act of 1995 is
an extremely serious threat to academic freedom.  I have posted to my home
page some examples of academic discourse that will become illegal under
the proposed act (and under some of the milder alternatives).  Also
available there is a text copy of the letter I wrote to a number of
senators at the beginning of April.  As Lord Jenkins of Hillhead points
out in his book, Academic Freedom (Routledge, 1993), academic discourse is
often offensive, but it is also protected speech for some very good
reasons.  The proposed bill trespasses into areas where the State has
historically been incompetent, and there is no evidence that it will be
competent this time. 

