My own belief - and that is all any of us can talk about, that
just about everyone involved is at substantial risk of being found
responsible in defamation.  

In general (prointed word) libel law, all those involved in the 
production and distribution of material are liable.  Only at the very
end - at the newsagents end does a defence begin to glimmer in the doom.
Here if he can show that he had no reasonable cause to think that the
material was defamatory, (I have not set out the full defence) he will
be excused.

My understanding therefore is that all those involved in transporting
libellous material are liable until they can show that this defence ought
to be applied to them.

Why is the defence given to the newsagent?  One major element is that
he does nothing to increase the number of copies of the defamatory
item.  Once you see that, then you can see that the 'end of the line'
supplier in e-space will have great difficulty in using this defence, 
since he vaunts the power of e-mail - the cheap automatic distribution
and propagation of one message over wide areas with many copies becoming 
available.

With that power comes the responsibility to make sure that he is not
creating new defamatory 'objects'

As a sysop, you can see this does not cheer me up, but I have yet to
be convinced that English law will take note of any of the US type
arguments which do creep into this discussion every now and then

David
