You seem to have difficulty following me.  Perhaps it is my fault, but
it doesn't matter.  I will try to set out the substance of my actions
against entities other than the authors so you are able to understand.

Suppose person X has access to usenet through his university.  Then
suppose that person X uses his access to post, through the computers
owned and controlled by the university, a defamatory posting.  Suppose
that I then put the university on notice that X is so doing and ask
them to take action to prevent further defamatory postings being published
from their system by the same user.  Suppose then that the university
takes no effective action to prevent repetition and as a result more
defamatory postings are made through said system by the same user.

In those circumstances I have a claim against the university as a 
publisher in UK law, and the new legislation will not change that
situation because the second and subsequent postings were not
innocently disseminated by the university as they had i) notice
and ii) the ability to prevent repetitions by various means at
their disposal.

Now do you see where I am coming from?  There is no need to start
talking about common cariiers, because the carriers are not parties
to the action.  The university, which is what I meant by the 
originating site, is liable in UK law at the present time and was
liable in UK law at the times the causes of these actions arose.
Under the new act, as I read the draft, they will continue to be 
liable in these circumstances.

I hope that helps you to understand, and avoids the need for you
to continue arguing over semantics.  As for your other points about
whether or not the law should be the way the law is, that is a 
matter for the legislators and the legal experts, not for me.  

