>s0lg@exnet.com (L Godfrey) writes:
>> ...
>>Suppose person X has access to usenet through his university.  Then
>>suppose that person X uses his access to post, through the computers
>>owned and controlled by the university, a defamatory posting.  Suppose
>>that I then put the university on notice that X is so doing and ask
>>them to take action to prevent further defamatory postings being published
>>from their system by the same user.  Suppose then that the university
>>takes no effective action to prevent repetition and as a result more
>>defamatory postings are made through said system by the same user.
>
>>In those circumstances I have a claim against the university as a 
>>publisher in UK law, and the new legislation will not change that
>>situation because the second and subsequent postings were not
>>innocently disseminated by the university as they had i) notice
>>and ii) the ability to prevent repetitions by various means at
>>their disposal.
>
>I have been following this discussion with interest, but there is one thing
>I don't understand. When you say you have informed the University that a
>particular poster is making defamatory postings how do you expect the
>University to judge whether that is indeed the case? That is, there is
>clearly a disagreement over a particular statement, and you are asking the
>University (or other service provider) to decide that you are correct in the
>absence of any evidence either way. If this were to be applied by service
>providers it could lead to the situation whereby anyone disagreeing with
>statements, articles, or a particular poster, could inform the provider and
>insist that they stop that poster from continuing to post. This I feel would
>have a harmful affect on the freedom of discussion on Usenet and other
>network media. 
>
>Surely in the absence of a proven legal case against the individual who
>posted the article in question the University or service provider should
>actively maintain a stance of neutrality to either side. 

Your point is well taken and goes to the heart of the way in which our law 
should develop. 

The technique of twisting the arm of publishers and distributors to suppress 
publication and distribution has become an unpleasant feature of the law of 
England and Wales (I wot not of Scottish law in this matter).

What set out to be a reasonble if powerful mechanism for the protection
of a few against serious defamation has, additionally,  become a notorious 
gagging device that is widely used by the incompetent (but retaining 
competent lawyers :-) ) or by the plainly criminal. By popular account, the 
late Robert Maxwell was one on whose behalf this mechanism was frequently 
used.

In the nature of the law, there is no going back from this unfortunate point at 
which we have arrived, other than by the creation of a new statute designed to 
overturn the precedents set in case law over the years.

We are now faced with the possibility of a massive increase in such gagging 
if it is extended to the providers of mass electronic communications. However,
this does not have to happen.

There would seem to be two alternatives. Firstly, that a bill is introduced 
that limits the recourse of a potential litigator against communications 
systems owners or at least provides the owners with a strong defence of 
innocent dissemination. Secondly, that a wise judge presented with a well 
argued case chooses to distinguish the nature and condition of electronic 
communications and the internet from the paper publishing that it only in some 
part resembles.  


Owen
