In article <tja.803189684@phobos> tja@citri.edu.au "Tim Arnold-Moore" writes:

>
>In response to this challenge
>>> >There is already a presumption of liability unless innocent dissemination 
>>> > can be shown. 
>>> 
>>> Would you provide a reference or references to this?
>
>s0lg@exnet.com (L Godfrey) suggested there was no reference.
>
>However:
>A republisher of defamatory material will be held liable if their role
>amounted to approval or adoption of the statement inferred from actual
>words with a major bias towards finding against republishers as they
>expose the material to a new audience.
>  ``Truth'' (NZ) Ltd v. Philip North Holloway [1960] 1 WLR 997, 1002-3;
>  Hepburn v. TCN Channel 9 [1983] 2 NSWLR 664; 
>  McCauley v. John Fairfax & Sons (1933) 34 SR (NSW) 339, 341-2;
>In particular the relayer of a telegraph message has been found liable
>for republishing it (most relevant for email).
>  Tobin v. City Bank (1878) 1 SCR NS (NSW) 267 (Full court of the
>  Supreme Court)
>Given the power of system administrators to `kill' news postings,
>analogies are easily drawn with the physical bulletin board cases for
>news and mailing list services.
>In particular the English case of _Byrne v. Deane_ [1937] 1 KB 818
>provides that the person who has the power to remove such items is
>liable if they are left there for a significant period of time.
>The recent case of _Stratton Oakmonth v. Prodigy_ Unreported24/May
>1995, New York Supreme Court and the _Cubby, Inc. v. CompuServe_ 776 F.
>Supp 135 (1991) both illustrate clearly that Internet-like services
>will be treated as publication and that service-providers can be
>republishers.
>
>Innocent dissemination is a defence _Emmens
>v. Pottle_ (1885) 16 QBD 354, 357-8, _Goldsmith v. Sperring_ [1977] 2
>All ER 566, 581-2.
>Defences are presumed not to operate unless raised, therefore
>liability is presumed unless the defence can be maintained.
>

Thank you for the intetesting list of references.

Far be it from me to take LG's part, but he was (I think) making the sensible 
point that there is no UK precedent in respect of electronic dissemination.

The nut of this discussion is whether or not electonic communication and 
the internet is to be regarded in law is indistinguishable from paper 
publishing and distribution. I assume you start from the assumption that that
decision has already been made?

>Godfrey's nemesis (sorry I lost the original posters name) suggested
>a defence might operate under the Telecommunications Act.
>I agree entirely that the scope of services covered by this
>legislation is deliberately limited and also very clear as the 
>basis for the exemption of telecoms providers is that they have a
>statutory obligation to provide the service (which is not the case for
>Internet providers) therefore they have some unique protections to
>compensate.
>If service providers are not included in the scope of the legislation, in
>particular email and news are not, then they are in the same position
>as everybody else i.e. no special protection.

In regard to the Telecommunications Act 1984, to which I referred, i do not 
believe that you are correct to say that the providers are under a statutory
obligation (to provide). This Act sets out who shall be the licensing 
authority, the nature of licences granted, the terms that licencees actual and 
prospective must meet and certain other sibsiidiary matter. There is no 
obligation on any party to take a licence as a PSTN supplier nor to continue 
to do so having become a licensee.

As there is no longer any statutory obligation here, I think that your 
suggestion of counterbalancing special privilege may be unwarranted. 
Accordingly, I see no reason why the sensible principles set out in s.44-45
(Offences) could not reasonably be extended to other providers of publid 
electronic communication systems and the internet. 

You will also note, no doubt that s.43(1)(b) of that Act makes it a criminal
offence for a person to send 'by those means, for the prpose of causing 
annoyance, inconvenience or needless anxiety to another, a message that he 
knows to be false or persistently makes use for that purpose of a public 
telecommunications system.

Reliance on this would seem to give good recourse to those genuinely hurt,
while weeding out the greedy, the incompetent, the criminal etc........

